WEBVTT

NOTE Ruwaidix source-grounded narration; original owner archive and labeled explanations.

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.335
In 2016, we met around an organic chemistry bench. Myrrh,

00:00:05.335 --> 00:00:09.188
nutmeg, extraction questions, and the suspicion that a

00:00:09.188 --> 00:00:13.521
traditional medicinal claim needed more than an interesting smell.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.911
It was easy to obtain an extract and much harder to say what it

00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:24.992
contained. Identifying a constituent would still be different

00:00:24.992 --> 00:00:27.303
from proving a useful biological effect.

00:00:29.978 --> 00:00:33.772
The group that became Chem Squad included seven chemistry

00:00:33.772 --> 00:00:37.987
graduates. There was no grand institution behind us. We kept

00:00:37.987 --> 00:00:42.202
bringing papers, arguments, and new questions back to the conversation.

00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:49.193
Around that period, our student chapter of the American Chemical

00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:53.283
Society at Qatar University was creating a different kind of

00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:56.964
laboratory: one open to more students and the public.

00:00:59.812 --> 00:01:03.556
We organized demonstrations and scientific discussions. Through

00:01:03.556 --> 00:01:07.300
chapter activities, twenty-nine students had opportunities to

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.114
train on instruments. The work was collective; the photographs

00:01:12.114 --> 00:01:13.719
deserve collective credit.

00:01:16.853 --> 00:01:20.128
Chemistry festivals taught me another form of precision:

00:01:20.128 --> 00:01:24.222
speaking clearly when the audience has every reason to question

00:01:24.222 --> 00:01:28.725
you, and resisting the urge to make a demonstration seem conclusive.

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:35.349
As years passed, we moved into different specialties. Some

00:01:35.349 --> 00:01:38.328
conversations continued through weekly meetings, and some

00:01:38.328 --> 00:01:43.433
through disagreements sent across a phone long after a class had ended.

00:01:46.228 --> 00:01:51.000
There is no final group photograph that explains all of it. What

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.976
the archive shows is simpler: people kept making time for

00:01:54.976 --> 00:01:57.759
scientific curiosity, even when nobody assigned it.

